# Double Taxation

Double taxation occurs when corporate profits are taxed at the entity level and then again when distributed to shareholders as dividends.

## How It Works
In a C Corporation, the business pays corporate income tax on its profits. When those profits are distributed to shareholders as dividends, the shareholders pay personal income tax on the dividend income. This results in the same money being taxed twice.

## Entity Types Affected
- **C Corporation**: Subject to double taxation by default
- **S Corporation**: Avoids double taxation through pass-through treatment
- **LLC**: Avoids double taxation by default (pass-through)

## Workarounds and Considerations
- S Corps and LLCs elect pass-through taxation to avoid this
- C Corps can retain earnings for reinvestment, deferring dividend tax
- Fringe benefits for shareholders can be structured tax-efficiently in C Corps
- Some jurisdictions have different corporate vs. pass-through tax rates

